ves of that rook until 57. f1a1 -> Rfa1? Actually f1 to a1. So Rfa1.\n58. d8a8 (B) -> Ra8? Black rook from d8 to a8.\n59. a7b6 (W) -> Bb6? Bishop from a7 to b6.\n60. a8a4 (Black) -> Ra4? Rook from a8 to a4.\n61. e2d2 (White) -> Kd2.\n\nNow it's Black's turn (move 62). We need to find the best move for Black.\n\nLet's set up the final position after White's 61st move (Kd2).\n\nLet's list all pieces and their positions.\n\nWhite pieces:\nKing: d2 (from e2)\nQueens: none (exchanged)\nRooks: a1 (from f1?), and maybe another rook? White had rooks on a1 and d1? Wait, move 17: a1d1 was White's move. White captured queen with rook from a1 to d1. Then later d1d8 (43) rook captures on d8. Then Black recaptures with a8d8. So White's d1-rook is gone. White's other rook was on h1 after castling? Actually after O-O, rooks are on f1 and d1? No, White castled kingside: e1g1 (23). So king on g1, rook on f1. Then later f1a1 (57) moved rook from f1 to a1. So White has one rook on a1.\nKnights: None? White's knights: Nc3 was captured on c3 (move 54). Nf3 captured on f6 (move 29). So no knights.\nBishops: White had Bc4 (moved to b3, then d5, then captured on c6? Move 53: d5c6 Bxc6). So that bishop is gone. White had Bg5 -> h4 -> f2 -> e3 -> a7 -> b6. So White has a bishop on b6.\nPawns: White pawns: a3 (move 19), b2 captured on c3 (move 55) so b-pawn gone? Actually b2c3 means b-pawn captured on c3, so now on c3. c4 was captured? White had c4, then Bxc4, then later? Wait, White played c4, then dxc4 Bxc4. So White's c-pawn is gone. d-pawn: d4 captured on c5 (move 15). e-pawn: e3, then e4 (move 37). f-pawn: f4, then f5 (move 33). g-pawn: still on g2? Let's check: g-pawn hasn't moved. h-pawn: hasn't moved.\nSo White pawns: a3, c3 (from b-pawn), e4, f5, g2, h2. And maybe e4? Wait, e3-e4 was played. So pawn on e4.\n\nLet's verify White's pawn structure:\na3\nb-pawn captured on c3 -> pawn on c3.\nc-pawn captured on c4? Actually c4 was played, then dxc4 Bxc4, so c-pawn was captured.\nd-pawn: d4