stled, f7 pawn is on f7. Then no moves of f-pawn. So f7 pawn still on f7? Wait, g7 pawn captured on f6. So Black's pawns: f7, g7 is gone (captured on f6), h7 moved to h5 (move 40).\nSo Black pawns: f7, e5, c6, h5. Also a-pawn? a7 was captured by bishop. b7 captured on c6. c7? c7 moved to c5, captured. d7? d7 moved to d5, captured. So Black pawns: c6, e5, f7, h5.\nKing: g7.\nBishop: f8.\nRook: a4.\n\nLet's double-check all pieces and squares.\n\nWhite:\nKing d2\nRook a1\nBishop b6\nPawns a3, c3, e4, f5, g2, h2.\n\nBlack:\nKing g7\nRook a4\nBishop f8\nPawns c6, e5, f7, h5.\n\nIs this correct? Let's verify captures and moves.\n\nMove 51: e3a7 (Be3xa7). So White bishop captured on a7, which was a black pawn. So a7 pawn gone.\nMove 52: d6b5 (Nd6-b5). Black knight to b5.\nMove 53: d5c6 (Bd5xc6). White bishop captures on c6. What was on c6? Black's bishop was on c6 from move 28. So Black's light-squared bishop captured.\nMove 54: b5c3 (Nb5xc3). Black knight captures on c3. White's knight was on c3? Yes, Nc3 was there. So White knight gone.\nMove 55: b2c3 (bxc3). White b-pawn captures on c3, so pawn on c3.\nMove 56: b7c6 (bxc6). Black b-pawn captures on c6. So Black pawn on c6, White bishop on c6 is captured? Wait, White bishop moved to c6 on move 53, capturing Black's bishop. Then Black plays b7c6, capturing that White bishop. So White's light-squared bishop is gone. Black pawn on c6.\nMove 57: f1a1 (Rf1-a1). White rook to a1.\nMove 58: d8a8 (Rd8-a8). Black rook to a8.\nMove 59: a7b6 (Ba7-b6). White bishop to b6.\nMove 60: a8a4 (Ra8-a4). Black rook to a4.\nMove 61: e2d2 (Kd2).\n\nNow it's Black's turn. We need the best move for Black.\n\nLet's analyze the position.\n\nMaterial:\nWhite: King, Rook, Bishop, 6 pawns (a3, c3, e4, f5, g2, h2) = 1+5+3+6 = 15? Wait, Rook=5, Bishop=3, pawns=6 -> total 14. King not counted.\nBlack: King, Rook, Bishop, 4 pawns (c6, e5, f7, h5) = 5+3+4 = 12.\nWhite is up two pawns (material advantage).\n\nPosition:\nWhite king on d2, somewhat exposed