lem statement, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- P_{{i, j, k}} is # or ..\n- The given polyominoes are connected. In other words, the squares that make up a polyomino can be reached from each other by following only the squares up, down, left, and right.\n- The given polyominoes are not empty.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n....\r\n###.\r\n.#..\r\n....\r\n....\r\n.###\r\n.##.\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n.##.\r\n.##.\r\n.##.\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\r\n\nThe figure below shows the shapes of the polyominoes corresponding to Sample Input 1.\n\nIn this case, you can fill the grid with them to satisfy the conditions in the problem statement by placing them as shown in the figure below.\n\nThus, the answer is Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n###.\r\n#.#.\r\n##..\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n####\r\n##..\r\n#...\r\n#...\n\nSample Output 2\n\nYes\r\n\nAs in the first polyomino in Sample Input 2, a polyomino may be in the shape of a polygon with a hole.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n##..\r\n#..#\r\n####\r\n....\r\n....\r\n##..\r\n.##.\r\n....\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\r\n.#..\n\nSample Output 3\n\nNo\r\n\nNote that the polyominoes may not be flipped over when filling the grid.\n\nSample Input 4\n\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\r\n....\r\n..#.\r\n....\r\n....\n\nSample Output 4\n\nNo\n\nSample Input 5\n\n....\r\n####\r\n#...\r\n#...\r\n....\r\n####\r\n...#\r\n..##\r\n....\r\n..##\r\n..#.\r\n..##\n\nSample Output 5\n\nNo\n\nSample Input 6\n\n###.\r\n.##.\r\n..#.\r\n.###\r\n....\r\n...#\r\n..##\r\n...#\r\n....\r\n#...\r\n#...\r\n#...\n\nSample Output 6\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n\n    # Read all input lines\n    lines = sys.stdin.read().strip().splitlines()\n    # Filter out any completely empty lines (shouldn't be any, but just in case)\n    lines = [line.strip() for line in lines if line.strip() != '']\n    if len(lines) < 12:\n        # According to the problem format, there are exactly 12 lines.\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n\n    pieces = []\n    for i in range(3):\n        grid = lines[i*4:(i+1)*4]\n        cells = []\n        for r in range(4):\n            for c in range(4):\n                if grid[r][c] == '#':\n                    cells.append((r, c))\n        pieces.append(cells)\n\n    # Quick check: total occupied cells must be exactly 16\n    total_cells = sum(len(p) for p in pieces)\n    if total_cells != 16:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n\n    # Generate all valid placements (translations + rotations) for each piece\n    all_placements = []\n    for piece_cells in pieces:\n        # Normalize piece to relative coordinates (top-left of bounding box at (0,0))\n        min_r = min(r for r, c in piece_cells)\n        min_c = min(c for r, c in piece_cells)\n        rel_cells = [(r - min_r, c - min_c) for r, c in piece_cells]\n\n        placements = set()\n        for rot in range(4):\n            # Rotate the relative coordinates\n            if rot == 0:\n                rotated = rel_cells\n            elif rot == 1:\n                rotated = [(c, -r) for r, c in rel_cells]\n            elif rot == 2:\n                rotated = [(-r, -c) for r, c in rel_cells]\n            else:  # rot == 3\n                rotated = [(-c, r) for r, c in rel_cells]\n\n            # Normalize the rotated piece\n            min_r = min(r for r, c in rotated)\n            min_c = min(c for r, c in rotated)\n            norm_rot = [(r - min_r, c - min_c) for r, c in rotated]\n\n            # Dimensions of the rotated bounding box\n            max_r = max(r for r, c in norm_rot)\n            max_c = max(c for r, c in norm_rot)\n            height = max_r + 1\n            width = max_c + 1\n\n            # Try all translations within the 4x4 grid\n            for dr in range(4 - height + 1):\n                for dc in range(4 - width + 1):\n                    placement = frozenset((r + dr, c + dc) for r, c in norm_rot)\n                    placements.add(placement)\n\n        all_placements.append(list(placements))\n\n    # Sort pieces by number of valid placements to reduce backtracking\n    all_placements.sort(key=len)\n\n    # Depth-first search with backtracking\n    def dfs(idx, occupied):\n        if idx == 3:\n            return len(occupied) == 16\n        for placement in all_placements[idx]:\n            if occupied & placement:\n                continue  # Overlap detected\n            if dfs(idx + 1, occupied | placement):\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    if dfs(0, frozenset()):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n```",
