      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def minCostToEqualizeArray(self, nums: List[int], cost1: int, cost2: int) -> int:\n        MOD = 10**9 + 7\n        n = len(nums)\n        if n == 1:\n            return 0\n        \n        S = sum(nums)\n        m = min(nums)\n        M = max(nums)\n        \n        # If using pairs is never beneficial\n        if cost2 >= 2 * cost1:\n            total_diff = n * M - S\n            return (total_diff * cost1) % MOD\n        \n        # For n=2, the cost function is linear increasing in T, optimal at T = M\n        if n == 2:\n            return ((M - m) * cost1) % MOD\n        \n        # n >= 3 and cost2 < 2 * cost1\n        # T0 is the threshold where B = total/2 in continuous sense\n        # ceil division: (num + den - 1) // den\n        T0 = (S - 2 * m + n - 3) // (n - 2)\n        \n        low = M\n        high = max(M, T0) + 10  # safe upper bound\n        \n        # Binary search for the first T >= M with total_minus_max >= total // 2\n        # f(T) = total_minus_max - total//2\n        # total_minus_max = (n-1)*T - S + m\n        # total = n*T - S\n        T2 = high\n        while low <= high:\n            mid = (low + high) // 2\n            total = n * mid - S\n            total_minus_max = total - (mid - m)\n            A = total // 2\n            if total_minus_max >= A:\n                T2 = mid\n                high = mid - 1\n            else:\n                low = mid + 1\n        \n        def cost(T: int) -> int:\n            total = n * T - S\n            max_diff = T - m\n            total_minus_max = total - max_diff\n            pairs = min(total // 2, total_minus_max)\n            return pairs * cost2 + (total - 2 * pairs) * cost1\n        \n        candidates = [M]\n        if T2 > M:\n            candidates.append(T2 - 1)\n        candidates.extend([T2, T2 + 1, T2 + 2])\n        \n        min_cost = min(cost(T) for T in candidates)\n        return min_cost % MOD\n```",
