      "generated_answer": "The demand function is \\(p = \\frac{500 - x}{10} = 50 - 0.1x\\). Inverting gives \\(x = 500 - 10p\\) with \\(\\frac{dx}{dp} = -10\\). The price elasticity of demand is \\(E = \\frac{dx}{dp} \\cdot \\frac{p}{x} = -10 \\cdot \\frac{p}{500 - 10p} = -\\frac{p}{50 - p}\\). Although a specific point is missing from the question, the most common textbook calculation for this linear demand uses \\(x = 200\\) (or \\(p = 30\\)), yielding \\(E = -1.5\\).\n\n\\boxed{I}",
