      "generated_answer": "Let’s work through the problem carefully.  \n\nWe want the number of labeled forests on 10 vertices with exactly 5 connected components, and vertices 1–5 are each in different components. That means each component has exactly one of these labeled vertices and includes some (possibly none) of the remaining 5 vertices {6,7,8,9,10}.  \n\nThis is equivalent to counting the number of ways to assign the remaining 5 vertices to the 5 labeled components and then, within each component, form a tree rooted at the designated vertex (1–5).  \n\nBy Cayley’s formula, if a component has \\( k \\) vertices (including its designated root), the number of labeled trees on those \\( k \\) vertices with the designated root is \\( k^{k-2} \\). Summing over all partitions of the 5 extra vertices into 5 labeled groups and multiplying the tree counts gives **50000**.  \n\nSo the correct option is:\n\n\\boxed{B}",
