    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9304",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Array A contains 256 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 4,096.\nArray B contains 512 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 8,192.\nAssume that only arrays A and B can be cached in an initially empty, physically addressed, physically tagged, direct-mapped, 2K-byte cache with an 8-byte block size. The following loop is then executed.\nfor (i = 0; i < 256; i++)\n        A[i] = A[i] + B[2*i];\nDuring the execution of the loop, how many bytes will be written to memory if the cache has a write-back policy?\n\nOptions: \nA. 512\nB. 1028\nC. 4096\nD. 1024\nE. 2000\nF. 5000\nG. 2048\nH. 1500\nI. 256\nJ. 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let’s go step-by-step.  \n\n**Cache details:**  \n- Direct-mapped  \n- 2KB = 2048 bytes of storage  \n- Block size = 8 bytes → Number of blocks = 2048/8 = 256 blocks  \n- Physically addressed, physically tagged.  \n\n**Arrays:**  \n- A: starts at 4096, 256 elements × 4 bytes = 1024 bytes total  \n- B: starts at 8192, 512 elements × 4 bytes = 2048 bytes total  \n- Offset between A and B is 4096 bytes; their address ranges don’t overlap in cache index initially, but we must map by modulo.  \n\n**Cache indexing:**  \n- 8-byte block → offset bits = 3.  \n- 256 blocks → index bits = 8.  \n- Tag = remaining bits.  \n\nA’s address:  \nBlock number for A[i] = (4096 + 4i) / 8.  \nSince 4096 / 8 = 512 → index = 512 mod 256 = 0. So all of A maps to index 0..127.  \n\nB’s address:  \nFor B[2i], address = 8192 + (2i × 4) = 8192 + 8i.  \n8192 / 8 = 1024 → index = 1024 mod 256 = 0. So these also map starting at index 0.  \n\n**Conflict:**  \nA and B accesses conflict in the same cache sets (index 0..127).  \n\n**Write-back policy effects:**  \n- Every store to A[i] will be to cache first.  \n- On conflict misses between A and B, dirty blocks from A will be evicted and written back to memory.  \n- Each A[i] is updated once; there are 256 writes × 4 bytes each = 1024 bytes total to memory upon eviction.  \n\nSo **the answer is 1024 bytes written to memory**, corresponding to option **D**.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{D}\n\\]",
