  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_computer science_9461",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider a computer system in which processes can request and release one or more resources. Once a process has been granted a resource, the process has exclusive use of that resource until it is released. If a process requests a resource that is already in use, the process enters a queue for that resource, waiting until the resource is available. Which of the following will NOT deal effectively with the problem of deadlock?\n\nOptions: \nA. Giving priorities to processes and ordering the wait queues by priority\nB. Numbering the resources and requiring that processes request resources in order of increasing number\nC. Having a process request all its required resources when it first begins, and restarting if it cannot obtain them all\nD. Allowing processes to request resources only in order of decreasing number\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Option A describes using priorities to order wait queues, which only determines which waiting process gets the resource next but does not prevent the circular wait condition necessary for deadlock. The other options are standard deadlock prevention techniques: requesting all resources at once (C) eliminates hold-and-wait, while hierarchical resource ordering, whether increasing (B) or decreasing (D), eliminates circular wait.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
