 current = 2.60 A\" is the current at rated conditions. We'll assume it's given as the current through the shunt field.\n\nWe need to compute efficiency = P_out / (P_out + total losses).\nTotal losses = No-load rotational losses + Stray-load loss + Armature copper loss + Series field copper loss + Commutating pole field copper loss + Shunt field copper loss.\n\nStray-load loss = 1% of output = 0.01 * 100,000 = 1000 W.\nRotational losses = 3840 W.\nWe need to find the currents.\nRated output power = 100 kW, V = 250 V.\nLoad current I_L = P_out / V = 100,000 / 250 = 400 A.\n\nIn long-shunt, armature current Ia = I_L + Ish = 400 + 2.60 = 402.60 A.\nSeries field current = Ia = 402.60 A.\nCommutating pole field current = Ia = 402.60 A (typically commutating poles are in series with armature? Yes, commutating-pole field is a series winding, so its current is Ia).\nShunt field current Ish = 2.60 A.\nShunt field copper loss = V * Ish? In long-shunt, shunt field is across the 250 V line, so loss = V * Ish = 250 * 2.60 = 650 W. (Alternatively, I^2 Rsh, but V*I is same.)\nArmature copper loss = Ia^2 * Ra = (402.6)^2 * 0.012.\nSeries field copper loss = Ia^2 * Rs = (402.6)^2 * 0.004.\nCommutating pole field copper loss = Ia^2 * Rc = (402.6)^2 * 0.004.\n\nCalculate Ia^2:\n402.6^2 = (400 + 2.6)^2 = 160000 + 2*400*2.6 + 2.6^2 = 160000 + 2080 + 6.76 = 162086.76.\n\nNow copper losses:\nArmature: 162086.76 * 0.012 = 1945.04112 W\nSeries field: 162086.76 * 0.004 = 648.34704 W\nCommutating: same as series = 648.34704 W\nShunt field = 250 * 2.60 = 650 W.\n\nTotal copper loss = 1945.04 + 648.35 + 648.35 + 650 = 3891.73 W approx.\n\nTotal losses:\nRotational: 3840\nStray-load: 1000\nCopper: 3891.73\nSum = 3840 + 1000 + 3891.73 = 8731.73 W.\n\nEfficiency = P_out / (P_out + losses) = 100000 / (100000 + 8731.73) = 100000 / 108731.73 ≈ 0.9197 ≈ 0.920.\n\nThis is about 0.920, not exactly one of the options. Options include: 0.820, 0.890, 0.900, 0.915, 0.847, 0.935, 0.928, 0.865, 0.880, 0.950. 