    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The Supreme Court has now ruled that older workers need no longer prove _____________ to win a job discrimination suit.\n\nOptions: \nA. Unfair treatment\nB. Discrimination\nC. Employment history\nD. Intention\nE. Economic loss\nF. Age bias\nG. Work environment hostility\nH. Health conditions\nI. Negative impact\nJ. Previous warnings\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Older workers filing age discrimination suits under the Age Discrimination in Employment Act (ADEA) were previously required to show intentional discrimination. The Supreme Court's 2005 ruling in *Smith v. City of Jackson* established that they could bring disparate impact claims, which do not require proof of discriminatory intent. Therefore, they no longer need to prove intention.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
