    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4506",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The secondarily altricial condition of modern human babies may have been an evolutionary solution, in that:\n\nOptions: \nA. it resulted in babies being born with fully formed teeth.\nB. it allowed for subsequent growth of the brain, since bipedalism had resulted in a narrowed birth canal in the female pelvis.\nC. it led to an increase in the size of the newborn's head, allowing for a larger brain.\nD. the brain size of hominids had not grown for more than a million years, making it difficult for babies to walk.\nE. it allowed babies to be born with an instinctive ability to swim.\nF. it allowed for an earlier development of language skills in babies.\nG. it allowed for bipedalism and independence from the mother at an earlier age.\nH. it allowed for growth of the brain to nearly adult size prior to birth, since bipedalism had resulted in a wider birth canal in the female pelvis.\nI. it allowed for the development of advanced motor skills at an earlier age.\nJ. it led to an increase in the gestation period allowing for the brain to fully develop before birth.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The secondary altricial condition in human infants refers to being born in a relatively helpless, underdeveloped state, which is widely explained by the \"obstetric dilemma.\" Bipedalism selected for a narrower pelvis, which constrained the birth canal. As a result, natural selection favored earlier birth when the infant's skull is still small enough to pass through, allowing for significant postnatal brain growth.\n\n**Correct Answer:**\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
