      "generated_answer": "The appropriateness and usefulness of a pedestrian survey in archaeology primarily depends on the visibility of artifacts, which is greatly enhanced by naturally exposed layers such as eroded surfaces, plowed fields, or cut banks where materials are visible on the ground. Options like weather, political stability, or the mere presence of assemblages are less direct determinants of whether this specific surface method is suitable for a given area. Therefore, exposed layers enabling direct observation of materials is the key factor.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
