      "generated_answer": "Dorothea Dix was a leading antebellum reformer whose advocacy for the mentally ill was deeply rooted in the humanitarian and moral reform impulses spurred by the religious revivals of the early nineteenth century. The Second Great Awakening inspired many individuals to pursue social reforms such as prison and asylum improvement, temperance, and abolition, making it the direct influence reflected in her 1843 testimony.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 383,
        "output_tokens": 484,
