    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1002",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: George was charged with the murder of a well- known criminal. The victim was found shot to death in his car. The only witness to the shooting was the victim's pet parrot, which was found hovering over the victim's body repeating over and over, \"George don't. . . George don't. \"To prove the identity of the victim's killer, the prosecution has a witness testify that about the time of the murder, he was in the area and heard the victim's parrot calling George's name. The best objection to the testimony is that it is\n\nOptions: \nA. leading the witness.\nB. privileged communication.\nC. speculative.\nD. opinion testimony.\nE. character evidence.\nF. irrelevant.\nG. an assertion by the witness.\nH. self-serving.\nI. circumstantial evidence.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The testimony relates to what the witness heard the parrot say. This is offered to prove the truth of the matter asserted (that George was the killer), but a parrot’s repetition is not a statement made by a person and cannot be cross-examined.  \n\nThus, the best objection is that it is **irrelevant** because it constitutes an out-of-court statement offered for its truth without falling under a hearsay exception and lacks the reliability needed for relevance.  \n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 225,
        "output_tokens": 101,
        "total_tokens": 326
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0007429800000000001,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1005",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Congress enacted a statute aimed at \"countries assisting or furnishing aid or support to nations or movements engaged in hostilities with the United States. \" Section 5 of that act authorized and directed the Treasury Department \"to issue orders barring entry into the United States any category of goods the sale of which in the United States is likely to improve the economic or balance of payments posture of an assisting country. \" The Secretary of State was authorized by Section 6 to define \"assisting countries. \" Pursuant to Section 5, the Treasury Department issued a regulation, which provided in part that:\"Imports of the following categories of goods from assisting countries are hereby prohibited: ¢ . (c) Bulk shipments for resale within the United States of books, pamphlets, flags, decorations or other symbols, excepting, however, scientific, technical, and literary works intended for scholarly purpose. . . \"The State Department designated a foreign country, as an \"assisting country,\" on the basis of its determination that medical supplies collected by public donation in this country had been sent to another country currently engaged in hostilities with the United States. As a consequence, the Treasury Department issued an order barring practically all products and goods from this foreign country into the United States. A distributor of state and foreign flags, has had a lucrative contract with the foreign country for the importation and sale of their flags in the United States. However, because of the Treasury Department's order, the foreign country is now barred from importing any of its flags into the United States. In an appropriate federal court, the distributor brings a suit against the Secretary of State and the Treasury Department to set aside the order barring the foreign country's imports on the grounds that it is inconsistent with the principles of our constitutional form of government. Which of the following is the most proper disposition of the distributor's suit by the federal court?\n\nOptions: \nA. Suit dismissed, because it presents a nonjusticiable political question.\nB. Suit dismissed, because it presents a moot issue.\nC. Suit dismissed, because there is no adversity between the distributor and the defendants.\nD. Suit upheld, because the act violates the distributor's First Amendment rights.\nE. Suit dismissed, because the distributor has not been directly harmed by the act.\nF. Suit dismissed, because the act is constitutional under the war powers of Congress.\nG. Suit dismissed, because the distributor cannot challenge a foreign policy decision.\nH. Suit upheld, because the act is an overreach of the Treasury Department's authority.\nI. Suit upheld, because the act is not narrowly tailored to achieve its purpose.\nJ. Suit dismissed, because the distributor does not have standing to bring this action.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The distributor is directly harmed by being unable to import flags, so there is standing and adversity, and the issue is not moot. However, the regulation’s categorical ban on flags while excepting scholarly works suggests a content-based restriction that is unlikely to survive First Amendment scrutiny, especially since flags are expressive items.  \n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 616,
        "output_tokens": 69,
        "total_tokens": 685
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00131196,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
