stematic genocide\". So one could argue the trials *did* include the crime of genocide, just not as a separate charge. If that's the case, G might be considered false by the author. But legally, genocide was not a crime in the Charter. The Genocide Convention was 1948. However, the UN General Assembly Resolution 96(I) in 1946 declared genocide a crime. The trials happened in 1945-46. At the time of the trials, genocide was not a codified international crime. So it's a matter of debate. Could be a trick.\n\nAnother thought: Maybe \"the war crimes trials\" refers to the trials conducted by the United Nations War Crimes Commission? No, \"Nuremberg and Tokyo\" are specified in A and F.\n\nLet's look at option D again: \"The war crimes trials were held after the Second World War, but before the creation of the United Nations.\" If the UN was created on October 24, 1945, and the Nuremberg trials began November 20, 1945, then D is false. But what if \"creation\" means the signing of the Charter (June 26) or the conclusion of the San Francisco Conference? The UN Charter was signed on June 26, 1945. The London Charter for the IMT was signed August 8, 1945. The trials started in November. So if creation means signing, D is false. If creation means the UN's first General Assembly (January 1946), then D is true. Some people consider the UN to have been \"created\" when it officially came into existence (Oct 24), which is after the London Charter but before the trials started. So \"held\" is the key word. The trials were held starting November 1945, after October 24. So D is strictly false.\n\nE: \"The war crimes trials were conducted by the United Nations.\" The IMT was established by the four Allied powers. The IMTFE was established by General MacArthur. The UN had no role in conducting them. The United Nations War Crimes Commission (UNWCC) was established in 1943 by the Allies, not the UN organization, and it did not conduct the trials. So E is false.",
