    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1356",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In transactions between merchants, what is not true about additional terms proposed in the acceptance?\n\nOptions: \nA. They become part of the contract only if both parties are merchants.\nB. They become part of the contract only if the offeree does not object within a reasonable time.\nC. They become part of the contract only if the offeree agrees to them within a reasonable time.\nD. Additional terms always become part of the contract.\nE. They become part of the contract only if the additional terms are in writing.\nF. They become part of the contract unless they materially alter the agreement.\nG. They become part of the contract only if the offeror sends a written confirmation within a reasonable time.\nH. They never become part of the contract.\nI. They become part of the contract unless the offer expressly limits acceptance to the terms of the offer.\nJ. They become part of the contract unless the offeror objects within a reasonable time to the additional terms.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are asked: \"In transactions between merchants, what is not true about additional terms proposed in the acceptance?\" The question is based on UCC § 2-207 (Battle of the Forms). We need to identify which statement is NOT true.\n\nLet's recall UCC §2-207(2): \"The additional terms are to be construed as proposals for addition to the contract. Between merchants such terms become part of the contract unless:\n(a) the offer expressly limits acceptance to the terms of the offer;\n(b) they materially alter it; or\n(c) notification of objection to them has already been given or is given within a reasonable time after notice of them is received.\"\n\nSo under UCC 2-207, between merchants, additional terms in acceptance become part of the contract UNLESS: (a) offer limits acceptance; (b) material alteration; (c) objection within reasonable time.\n\nWe need to find the option that is NOT true.\n\nLet's evaluate:\nA. \"They become part of the contract only if both parties are merchants.\" - Not true; the rule for additional terms becoming part applies only if both are merchants, but it says \"only if both parties are merchants.\" That is a condition (they do become part only if both are merchants; if one is not, they are proposals). But wait, the question says \"In transactions between merchants\", so we are already in that context. The statement \"They become part of the contract only if both parties are merchants\" is a true statement in general, but within the context \"transactions between merchants\", it might be considered extraneous or true. But it says \"only if\", which implies it's a necessary condition. In fact, they do only become part if both are merchants. So A is true? Wait, the UCC says \"Between merchants such terms become part of the contract unless...\" So if both parties are merchants, they become part unless certain exceptions. But if they are not both merchants, they do not become part automatically. So A is a true statement? However, the wording \"only if both parties are merchants\" means they become part only when both are merchants, which is true. But the question asks \"what is not true\". Let's check all.\n\nB. \"They become part of the contract only if the offeree does not object within a reasonable time.\" - Offeree is the one sending the acceptance with additional terms? Wait, terminology: Offeror makes offer; Offeree sends acceptance with additional terms. Under 2-207(2)(c), the terms become part unless \"notification of objection to them has already been given or is given within a reasonable time after notice of them is received.\" The objection is by the offeror, not the offeree. Offeree is the one proposing the terms. So B says \"only if the offeree does not object\". That's wrong; it's the offeror who might object. So B is not true.\n\nC. \"They become part of the contract only if the offeree agrees to them within a reasonable time.\" - The offeree is proposing them, so offeree already agrees. This doesn't make sense. Not true.\n\nD. \"Additional terms always become part of the contract.\" - False, there are exceptions.\n\nE. \"They become part of the contract only if the additional terms are in writing.\" - Not necessarily required to be in writing to become part under UCC? The acceptance itself is a written confirmation or acceptance. But additional terms don't have to be in writing? Actually, §2-207 applies to \"a definite and seasonable expression of acceptance... which states terms additional to or different from those offered...\" It doesn't require the additional terms to be in writing separately. It just says \"operates as an acceptance\". Could the acceptance be oral? 2-207 doesn't explicitly limit to writings, but usually it's about forms. Still, it's not \"only if in writing\". So E is probably false.\n\nF. \"They become part of the contract unless they materially alter the agreement.\" - This is true under 2-207(2)(b).\n\nG. \"They become part of the contract only if the offeror sends a written confirmation within a reasonable time.\" - The offeror doesn't need to send a confirmation. False.\n\nH. \"They never become part of the contract.\" - False.\n\nI. \"They become part of the contract unless the offer expressly limits acceptance to the terms of the offer.\" - True under 2-207(2)(a).\n\nJ. \"They become part of the contract unless the offeror objects within a reasonable time to the additional terms.\" - True under 2-207(2)(c) (objection by offeror).\n\nWe need the one that is NOT true. Several are not true: B, C, D, E, G, H. But maybe the question is a standard multiple-choice test where only one is the correct \"not true\" in the specific context of the UCC rule? Let's re-read the options carefully. It's possible the question is a typical law school or bar exam question. In many such questions, there might be a single answer that is \"not true\" about additional terms between merchants under 2-207. Let's think: The general rule is additional terms become part of the contract UNLESS:\n(a) offer limits acceptance;\n(b) material alteration;\n(c) objection within reasonable time.\n\nSome statements are true: F, I, J are true statements.\nA: \"They become part of the contract only if both parties are merchants.\" This is generally true (the rule only applies between merchants). But the question specifies \"In transactions between merchants\", so the premise already is that both are merchants. The statement A says \"only if both parties are merchants\", which is a true condition, but within the given context it's already satisfied. Is it \"not true\"? Technically it is true that they become part only if both are merchants. So A is true, though redundant.\n\nB: \"only if the offeree does not object within a reasonable time.\" Offeree is the one accepting, they proposed the terms. Objection is by offeror. So B is not true.\n\nC: \"only if the offeree agrees to them\" - offeree proposed them, so they already agree. Nonsense. 